;; spec:   stdlib §4.3 (row abs: "traps on the minimum of an integer type"), §4.10 (row Math/abs)
;; expect: trap
;; trap:   integer overflow in neg at i8
;; roots:  ../../lib
;; covers: Math/abs
;; The magnitude of the smallest integer of a type does not fit the type, so Math/abs traps where Java's
;; Math.abs hands the minimum back unchanged (a result that does not fit is a trap, types §2.12). The
;; width is the operand's: -128 is the minimum of an i8, and the trap names the width. The same at i64
;; is case 679; the neighbours of both minimums, which do not trap, are in case 654.
(ns main (:use fib.core) (:require [Math :as Math]))

(defun main () -> i64
  (zext i64 (Math/abs -128i8)))
